{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to EDPB Opinion 28-2024, what two main types of risks must be insignificant for an AI model trained on personal data to be considered anonymous?", "answer": "Both the likelihood of direct extraction of personal data and the likelihood of obtaining such data from queries must be insignificant.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "it may be possible to infer 28 information from the model, such as membership inference, in realistic scenarios. Therefore, for a SA to agree with the controller that a given AI model may be considered anonymous, it should check at least whether it has received sufficient evidence that, with reasonable means: (i) personal data, related to the training data, cannot be extracted 29 out of the model; and (ii) any output produced when querying the model does not relate to the data subjects whose personal data was used to train the model."}, {"page": 16, "quote": "for an AI model to be considered anonymous, using reasonable means, both (i) the likelihood of direct (including probabilistic) extraction of personal data regarding individuals whose personal data were used to train the model; as well as (ii) the likelihood of obtaining, intentionally or not, such personal data from queries, should be insignificant 37 for any data subject."}]},
  {"question": "What four key areas should SAs evaluate regarding AI model design to assess anonymity claims under EDPB Opinion 28-2024?", "answer": "SAs should evaluate selection of sources, data preparation and minimisation, methodological choices regarding training, and measures regarding outputs.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "The application and effectiveness of four key areas (identified below) should be considered in this regard."}]},
  {"question": "In EDPB Opinion 28-2024, what does 'regurgitation' mean in the context of generative AI?", "answer": "Regurgitation refers to outputs that directly relate to training data.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "For an AI system based on generative AI, regurgitation corresponds to the situation where outputs would directly relate to training data."}]},
  {"question": "What principle in Article 5(2) GDPR is highlighted as requiring controllers to demonstrate compliance in EDPB Opinion 28-2024?", "answer": "The accountability principle.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "Accountability principle (Article 5(2) GDPR) - This principle provides that the controller shall be responsible for, and be able to demonstrate, compliance with the GDPR."}]},
  {"question": "What must be documented to show that an AI model's processing is necessary for a legitimate interest under EDPB Opinion 28-2024?", "answer": "Controllers must document that the purpose cannot reasonably be fulfilled by other means and that the processing is necessary.", "evidence": [{"page": 22, "quote": "Recital 39 GDPR clarifies that 'Personal data should be processed only if the purpose of the processing could not reasonably be fulfilled by other means'. According to the CJEU and previous EDPB guidance, the condition relating to the necessity of the processing should be examined in light of the fundamental rights and freedoms of the data subjects, and in conjunction with the data minimisation principle enshrined in Article 5(1)(c) GDPR 56 ."}]},
  {"question": "How does EDPB Opinion 28-2024 describe the relationship between AI models and AI systems in the AI Act?", "answer": "AI models are essential components but do not constitute AI systems on their own; they require further components to become AI systems.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "'Although AI models are essential components of AI systems, they do not constitute AI systems on their own. AI models require the addition of further components, such as for example a user interface, to become AI systems. AI models are typically integrated into and form part of AI systems' 18 ."}]}
]}
